export abstract anchor FactoryOperations:
    semanticVocabulary:
        Agents change factory state only through defined semantic operations,
        not raw record updates. Ordinary code validates authority, dependency
        rules, and idempotency. The required vocabulary covers order and plan
        changes, execution reporting, engineer input, evidence, liveness, and
        progress.
    operationEnvelope:
        Every operation request carries a request identifier, operation name,
        actor, target, payload, and the expected order aggregate version for a
        mutation of an existing order. Creating an order aggregate presents an
        absence or uniqueness precondition instead. Creating or changing a
        record inside an existing order still presents the aggregate version
        plus any record-level precondition. Step and attempt versions and answer
        generations are payload preconditions. Factory-internal shared-state
        operations use their own record versions under the single writer lock.
        Every response carries a status, a result or error, and the current
        aggregate version. A conflict returns the current version and applies
        nothing. Idempotency replay precedes version comparison; the expected
        version is part of the canonical fingerprint; a corrected retry uses a
        new request identifier. Reads never conflict and report a committed
        snapshot and its time. Cross-order effects decompose into per-order
        operations with a correlation identifier and recorded per-order
        outcomes; a partial decomposition reports the committed and failed
        orders for recovery. Retries reuse the request identifier and return
        the original result. The transport is a project choice, recorded in the
        descriptor.
    actorClasses:
        Actors are the engineer, factory code, agents, and auditors. Factory
        code is trusted for internal controls. The engineer may act across the
        factory. An agent may act on its assigned step and the work beneath it;
        cross order or higher-tier actions follow revision authority.
        Auditors are read-only and perform no operation; their reads follow
        FactoryObservability.readAccess. Every operation is checked against
        these classes and the matrix in FactorySecurity.authorizationMatrix.
        Authentication stays project-specific.
    coreRecords:
        The canonical core records are order, step, attempt, result, question,
        assumption, and session. Cancellation requests, authorized scope
        records, jobs, leases, reservations, queue entries, dependency edges,
        artifacts, delivery receipts, and successor links are supporting records
        whose required fields are defined by the sections that govern them.
        An order carries its identifier, source prompt or specification
        reference, state, phase, priority, delivery intent, and timestamps. A
        step carries its identifier, order, objective and brief, expected output
        and completion criteria, dependency identifiers, state, required flag,
        assigned agent or model, worktree and branch when it writes files,
        attempt count, estimate, and result reference. An attempt carries its
        identifier, step, number, parent attempt or null, launch request
        identifier, session references, state, start and end times, end cause,
        class, retry-counted flag, effect key, evidence, and worktree when it
        writes files.
        A result carries its identifier, step and attempt, outcome, summary,
        evidence and artifact references, checks run, completion assessment, and
        the question generations and assumption records it relies on. A
        question carries its identifier, related order or step, asker, context,
        state, decision key, generation, answer, and blocking scope. An
        assumption carries its statement, provenance, scope, owner, state, and
        timestamps. A session carries its identifier, harness, external session
        reference, step attempt, discovery variant or token when applicable,
        epoch and per-source signal history, liveness state and last signal
        time, and last activity time.
        Every record carries a version for optimistic concurrency and
        timestamps. Fields defined as required by the governing section are
        required; conditional fields are null until they apply. Implementations
        may add more.
    orderAndPlan:
        - submit an order
        - revise an order's scope, priority, or delivery intent
        - add or revise a step
        - declare or remove a dependency
        - supersede a step after an authorized revision
        - propose a plan revision, including conflict resolution
    executionReporting:
        - report a step outcome as done, failed, or blocked
        - request help from a higher tier
        - escalate a question to the engineer
        - record a build or check result
        - attach evidence or artifacts to a step or order
    lifecycleAndAccounting:
        - request cancellation within the project's authority rules
        - send a heartbeat when the harness or integration supports one
        - report progress and an estimated completion time
    notAgentOperations:
        Dispatching and claiming work, allocating resources and accounts, and
        starting, shutting down, or importing work remain factory or engineer
        operations. Agents do not own the general orchestration loop.
    unresolvedOperationDetails:
        Cancellation authority, the effect of cancellation on a running harness
        session, and how progress estimates and completion times are produced,
        represented, and trusted remain open. Implementations must not present
        an estimate as a guarantee.
